---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('attentive')!;
const b = appBySlug('aftership-email')!;
const path = '/compare/attentive-vs-aftership-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/attentive-vs-aftership-email');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/attentive-vs-aftership-email');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is this Attentive SMS versus AfterShip email?",
    "answer": "No. Attentive documents abandonment email journeys, and AfterShip Email documents email and SMS flow actions. Confirm access and channel eligibility in each offered account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does AfterShip Tracking include this recovery workflow?",
    "answer": "Tracking ownership does not establish an Email marketing setup. Verify the separate product, events and integration needed for any delivery or returns suppression."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which AfterShip filters should I inspect?",
    "answer": "The checkout reminder guide documents minimum checkout value and recent-order conditions. Exercise their boundaries and inspect waiting messages after purchase."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does the $620 model establish?",
    "answer": "Only hypothetical gross profit: $378.20 at full price versus $291.40 after a 14% code, before other variable costs. No observed merchant outcome is asserted."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Compare two email and SMS recovery candidates through checkout eligibility, offer approval and queue control. AfterShip Email is a marketing product; owning AfterShip Tracking does not establish the same recovery setup.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Attentive", "reason": "Demonstrate cart-to-checkout journey priorities and triggered-email access."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "AfterShip Email", "reason": "Evaluate checkout-value and recent-order filters in the marketing flow itself."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>{a.name}</th><th>{b.name}</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Email scope</td><td>Cart and Shopify checkout abandonment email journeys.</td><td>Shopify checkout reminder flows with value and recent-order filters.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS scope</td><td>Demonstrate eligibility and the SMS journey offered to your account.</td><td>Documented Shopify SMS recovery; verify plan and event limitations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Separate systems</td><td>Reconcile other marketing senders and service conversations.</td><td>Inspect Email flows separately from Tracking and Returns notifications.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>Quote required email access, SMS volume and support.</td><td>Quote required Email/SMS features and any separate AfterShip products.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Two marketing candidates, not a channel split</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408727834772-Emails-in-journeys">Attentive documents email steps</a> for cart and Shopify checkout abandonment, with access arranged through its email team. <a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440875-how-to-increase-conversions-with-abandoned-checkout-emails">AfterShip Email documents</a> checkout reminders with minimum checkout value and recent-order filters. Checked November 25, 2026. These facts do not establish that one is deeper or more advanced. Use the same checkout brief and inspect the actual account features.</p>
<h2>Keep Tracking and Returns out of the feature shortcut</h2>
<p>AfterShip’s help center distinguishes Email &amp; SMS, Tracking and Returns. A tracking notification or return status is not proof of a marketing checkout trigger. If delivery or return data must pause recovery, identify the exact event source, customer/order match and integration supplying it. Assign an owner to verify that connection. Compare the Email product directly even when your store already uses another AfterShip product.</p>
<h2>Exercise the filter boundary</h2>
<p>In a proposed AfterShip Email exercise, choose your own minimum basket value, then submit checkouts below, at and above it. Add a recent purchase while a reminder waits and inspect the configured order-count condition and queue. In Attentive, demonstrate entry and exit between cart and checkout journeys with the same shoppers. Change basket quantity and inspect the resulting recovery link. These are proposed checks, not observed tests; a filter being available does not establish that every waiting send rechecks it.</p>
<h2>Approve subscription and bundle details</h2>
<p>For a subscription toggle left on, have the merchandising owner approve how the reminder describes recurring charges and verify the destination retains the intended purchase type. For a failed bundle discount, check Shopify combinations and ensure a signup offer does not stack unexpectedly. For gift-message abandonment, confirm the field survives the link. A financing question should route to support without promising approval. Keep content approval separate from permission to enable the flow.</p>
<h2>Check SMS event history and competing queues</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440894-how-to-enable-abandoned-cart-sms">AfterShip SMS guide</a> limits recovery to Shopify checkout context and lists plan requirements. Its historical-event limitations must be checked before expecting old abandonments to enter a new flow. <a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440861-how-to-set-up-a-flow-with-aftership-email">The flow guide</a> describes email/SMS actions and splits. Demonstrate the eligible channel, market settings and event history in both candidates. Assign one recovery owner, reconcile Shopify and other providers’ waiting messages, and replay an event to check deduplication. Recheck purchase, stock and permission before each action; do not infer cross-product queue cancellation from the AfterShip name.</p>
<h2>Model the incentive without inventing a result</h2>
<p>Illustrative $620 basket at 61% gross margin: goods cost is $241.80 and full-price gross profit is $378.20. A 14% code removes $86.80, leaving $533.20 revenue and $291.40 gross profit. Subtract payment, fulfillment, messaging and returns to estimate contribution. This is not evidence of recovery lift or a prescribed discount. For an unavailable size or shipping objection, resolve the actual issue before approving an incentive.</p>
<h2>Decide from a controlled release</h2>
<p>Obtain current quotes for the same eligible audience, email sends, SMS destinations and implementation support. Include separate Tracking or Returns costs only when your design needs them. Authenticate senders, route replies and release a limited eligible segment after approval and queue checks. Compare deduplicated orders and contribution over the same window; randomization needs an allocation and duration justified by traffic. Record a pause owner for broken links, duplicate offers or failed purchase exits. Select from demonstrated requirements, with no third-party winner or unsupported rating.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</div></section></BaseLayout>
